<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Quintus Tullius Cicero</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">102–<a href="/years/43-bce">43 BCE</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/1st-century-bce">1st century BCE</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Quintus Tullius Cicero was a Roman politician and soldier, the younger brother of the orator <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cicero">Marcus Tullius Cicero</a>. As one of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/julius-caesar">Julius Caesar</a>'s <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/legatus">legates</a> in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gaul">Gaul</a>, in 54 BCE he held his winter camp in the territory of the Nervii against a prolonged siege until Caesar relieved him. He was killed in the proscriptions of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/second-triumvirate">Second Triumvirate</a> in 43 BCE.</p></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Early life and background</h2><p>Quintus Tullius Cicero was born into a well-to-do equestrian family of Arpinum, about 100 kilometres south-east of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rome">Rome</a>. His father arranged for him to be educated with his brother Marcus in Rome, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/athens">Athens</a> and probably <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rhodes">Rhodes</a> in 79 to 77 BCE. Around 70 BCE he married Pomponia, the sister of Marcus's friend Titus Pomponius Atticus, a woman of strong personality. The marriage was long and disharmonious, and he divorced her in late 45 BCE despite several attempts by Marcus to reconcile them. They had a son, also named Quintus, born in 66 BCE.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Career</h2><p>Quintus was aedile in 66 BCE, praetor in 62 BCE and propraetor of the province of Asia for three years from 61 to 59 BCE. In <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/asia">Asia</a> he was honest in a post where many <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/roman-empire">Romans</a> amassed private property, but he inflicted old-fashioned punishments, such as drowning a convicted parricide in a sack. During the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gallic-wars">Gallic Wars</a> he served as a legate under Caesar, accompanied him on his second expedition to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/britain">Britain</a> in 54 BCE and survived a Nervian siege of his camp during the revolt of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ambiorix">Ambiorix</a>.</p><p>Caesar described him as a brave soldier who rallied his legion at a critical moment. In 51 BCE he served as legate under his brother, governor of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cilicia">Cilicia</a>. In Caesar's civil war he supported the Pompeian faction, and later obtained Caesar's pardon.</p><p>He had an impulsive temperament and fits of cruelty in operations, which <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/roman-empire">Romans</a> of the time frowned on. Marcus wrote to Atticus in 51 BCE that he dared not leave him alone. Quintus enjoyed Greek tragedy and wrote four tragedies in the Greek style, among them Troades, Erigona and Electra, all lost. Three letters to Tiro survive, and the Commentariolum Petitionis, a handbook on electioneering, which Andrew Lintott has argued is his, though its authenticity has been questioned.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Death and legacy</h2><p>The brothers were close, apart from a serious disagreement during Caesar's dictatorship from 49 to 44 BCE, and Marcus's letters to Quintus show their affection. When the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/second-triumvirate">Second Triumvirate</a> proscribed Quintus, his son and his brother in 43 BCE, Quintus fled from Tusculum with Marcus, then went home for travelling money. His son hid him and refused to reveal the hiding place even under torture. Hearing of this, Quintus gave himself up to try to save his son, but father, son and Marcus were all killed in 43 BCE.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Quintus Tullius Cicero” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>In 54 BCE they besieged <b>Quintus Cicero</b>'s winter camp.</blockquote><a href="/state/nervii">Nervii</a></li></ul></section>
<section class="enc-where enc-born"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Born and died</h2><p class="enc-born-p">Born in Arpino, in what is now <a href="/countries/italy">Italy</a>. Died in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rome">Rome</a>, in what is now <a href="/countries/italy">Italy</a>. <span class="enc-born-src">Places as Wikidata records them.</span></p><figure class="st-locator enc-loc"><a href="/atlas?at=41.65,13.61&amp;year=-64" aria-label="Arpino on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="4882 680 900 540" role="img" aria-label="Map: the birthplace of Quintus Tullius Cicero"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M5332 950h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M5332 950h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks the birthplace. <a href="/atlas?at=41.65,13.61&amp;year=-64">What was here in 64 BCE? &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></section>
